<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> What distinguishes television's conditions from cinema's?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Cinema is designed for concentrated viewing in a dark room; television was designed for the living room, with interruptions and divided attention.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Television is always silent.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">Cinema is always shorter.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Television has no images.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. These conditions shaped the art form: television developed the self-contained episode, the recurring cast, and the cliffhanger.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Think about where each is watched. The setting and expected attention differ.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> What is the difference between an episodic series and a serial?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">They are the same thing.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">An episodic series has self-contained episodes; a serial tells a continuous story across many episodes or seasons.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">An episodic series is longer.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">A serial has no characters.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The serial form has become important in what critics call prestige television.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Episodic means each episode stands alone. A serial continues from episode to episode.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> What does the serial form allow that a two-hour film cannot?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">An extended time frame in which to develop character and theme with great depth.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">A shorter running time.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">No characters at all.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">A simpler plot.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The Sopranos, The Wire, and Downton Abbey use this extended time to deepen their worlds.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The advantage of a serial is time. Seasons of episodes give room to develop characters and themes.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> Video art usually</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">is made only for broadcast.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">tells conventional stories for a mass audience.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">cannot be shown in galleries.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">uses the moving image for non-commercial, often experimental purposes, and typically avoids narrative.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. In its goals it is closer to painting and sculpture than to television, though it uses the same technology.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Video art is not entertainment. It uses video as an artist's medium.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> What is distinctive about Bill Viola's The Crossing?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">A news broadcast.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">A two-channel installation in which one man is consumed by fire and the same man by water, in extreme slow motion.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">A cartoon about animals.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">A fast comedy.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The slowness turns a brief violent event into a sustained meditation on transformation, destruction, and renewal.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The work shows two events, fire and water, in extremely slow motion across two screens.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> How does the form of The Wire mirror its content?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">It avoids real settings.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Each episode neatly ends with a solved crime.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">Its serial structure and unresolved cases reflect the self-perpetuating systems it depicts, which resist individual efforts to change them.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">The show has only one character.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The show resists the satisfying resolutions of episodic television, just as the institutions it examines resist reform.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The Wire does not resolve its cases neatly. That open-endedness is a formal choice that matches its subject.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> In Nam June Paik's TV Buddha, what do we see?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">A scripted drama.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">A statue of the Buddha facing a camera and a monitor that shows its own live image, in an endless feedback loop.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">A documentary about a monk.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">A news broadcast.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. There is no narrative, no editing, and no progression: a contemplative figure contemplating its own electronic image.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The work is a situation: a statue, a camera, and a monitor connected in a loop.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> Why are many video artworks designed to loop?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Because they are too short.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">Because looping saves money.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">So viewers can enter at any point and stay as long as they like, instead of watching from a fixed beginning to a fixed end.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">Because gallery visitors dislike endings.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Looping contradicts the fixed running time of a film or television episode and changes how a viewer's attention works.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Looping removes the fixed beginning and end. Think about how that changes how you enter the work.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> What did Paik's Global Groove imagine?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">A future in which viewers can tune in to any television station in the world, presented as a fast collage of music, dance, and images.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">A single channel of news.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">A silent film.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">A world without television.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Paik manipulated television signals and mixed found footage to make television itself an artist's medium.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Paik's work is a collage of images from around the world. It imagines viewers choosing among all the world's channels.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> A typical half-hour sitcom like I Love Lucy follows which structure?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">No plot at all.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">A single unbroken shot.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">An unresolved cliffhanger across ten seasons.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">A problem, escalating complications, and a resolution within one episode.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The tidy structure suited the living-room schedule, and repetition and escalation build the jokes.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Sitcoms are episodic. Each half hour sets up a problem, makes it worse, and solves it.</div>
  </div>
</div>

